while implementing a distributed cache I wanted to make sure we could pin a sufficiently large memory chunk in RAM at all times and manage swap in / swap outs from it.
<aka get the OS out of the picture for when/what to swap>

Before studying what OSes do lets setup our expectations then we can compare different OSes for a better match:
<a> Getting an in memory partition lets call this (A) 
    DESIRED ATTRIBUTES:
    <i>  preferrably contiguous memory
    <ii> using large pages to minimize dTLB misses

<b> Getting a Intention Log partition to persistent storage. Bottomline it will do write appends not write in place.
    DESIRED ATTRIBUTES:
    <i>  writes should be non blocking as much as possible. Only fsync should block
    <ii> but <i> should not come at the expense of swapping out anon pages. Else we could knock out 
         code/heap/stack pages using (A) defeating its entire purpose.


<c> getting efficient swapins from persistent storage to (A)
    DESIRED ATTRIBUTES:
    <i>  Reads should be as fast as possible meaning should use whatever buffering + readaheads required to keep the underlying
         device busy at line rate. Dont ask me to provide alignment I dont know and dont want to know let driver + I/O device
	 do that.
   <ii>  It should not knock out anon pages
   <iii> But this should be able to use as much free memory as is available. Since these are non dirty pages when required they can be
         trashed quickly.

    Reason I m rejecting DIRECT I/O being precisely reasons <i> and <iii>. I want readaheads and I want to use buffering to keep the underlying
    device busy. I m more than happy to do the extra mem2mem I believe thats not the bottleneck for large I/O copies from persistent storage
    (in our case from a NAS)

==============
On Linux:
==============

<a> is easily served using hugetlb pages (refer hugetlb under tutorials/linux)
    We could have used ramfs but hugetlb offers you better tlb hit rates (refer hugetlb to see how much dTLB hits matter)

<b> <i>  is made possibly by default .. writes hit out of the page cache (we re not using fadvise or DIRECT I/O)

    <ii> yes this is a problem as noticed without effective user level write throttling cpus ability to dirty the pages for large
    	 copies will almost always beat writeback rate. 
	 	 vm.dirty_ratio  = 2
         This makes sure that the amount of outstanding dirty pages at a given time is not more than 2% of total RAM. Of course you 
	 can play around with this number but this ensures pages are not dirtied beyond 2% and if they are writes start become blocking
	 and the thread invoking write starts helping with writeback (exactly what we want use free buffer as much as possible but
	 not at the expense of anon pages)
	     	  vm.swappiness  = 0
	 This was done as a precaution. My *understanding* is that with this set anon pages dont get kicked out unless memory has run out

<c>  This comes out of the box. Reads never evict anon pages (atleast on 2.6.32-25-generic) and when theres free memory it uses it all up.
     I m told internally they do a good job with readaheads for read patterns that are sequential in nature (as is ours)

